insert into public.generated_questions
  (id, skill_id, difficulty, stem, choices, correct_answer, solution, render_mode, prompt, status, template_id, sat_alignment, passage_id)
select v.id::uuid, v.skill_id, v.difficulty::public.question_difficulty, v.stem, v.choices, v.correct_answer, v.solution,
       v.render_mode, v.prompt, 'published', null, null, null
  from (values
  ('354b2400-d74a-5232-af44-59a2715b6b08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Mara felt ambivalent about the summer internship: she was excited to work in a real laboratory, but she dreaded giving up her weekends." As used in the sentence, "ambivalent" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"having mixed feelings","is_correct":true},{"text":"afraid of science","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"skilled with equipment","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"completely uninterested","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$having mixed feelings$q$, $q$The colon introduces two opposite reactions, excitement and dread, so ambivalent means having mixed feelings. "Completely uninterested" fails because Mara is clearly excited about part of the internship.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('aa1439b2-4274-55e7-9876-7c387379520e', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Rather than waiting for a perfect design, the engineers took a pragmatic approach and built a simple bridge that could open before the flood season." As used in the sentence, "pragmatic" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"practical and focused on results","is_correct":true},{"text":"expensive and slow to complete","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"idealistic and focused on perfection","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"artistic and focused on beauty","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$practical and focused on results$q$, $q$The contrast clue "rather than waiting for a perfect design" shows the engineers chose what would work in time, so pragmatic means practical. "Idealistic and focused on perfection" describes exactly the approach they rejected.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('9629c70e-0002-5114-b15b-4309b087c1b2', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The link between the two events was tenuous at best; the only connection anyone could find was that both happened on a Tuesday." As used in the sentence, "tenuous" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"related to time","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"deliberately hidden","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"firmly established","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"very weak or slight","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$very weak or slight$q$, $q$The explanation after the semicolon shows the only connection is a trivial one, so tenuous means very weak. "Related to time" grabs onto the detail about Tuesday but misses that the word describes how flimsy the link is.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('8376158b-1892-52ce-9512-2d2487172c46', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentences. "At first, historians doubted the sailor's diary. Then tax records and a second ship's log were found that corroborated nearly every date he had written down." As used in the sentence, "corroborated" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"copied","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"contradicted","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"translated","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"confirmed","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$confirmed$q$, $q$The shift from "at first, historians doubted" to new records matching "nearly every date" shows the records supported the diary, so corroborated means confirmed. "Contradicted" is the opposite, since the doubt is being cleared up, not deepened.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('5bd1b837-43c8-5a99-a292-89f80aefc8c2', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Plastic packaging has become so ubiquitous that researchers have found fragments of it in deep ocean trenches, mountain snow, and city air." As used in the sentence, "ubiquitous" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"found only in water","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"rarely seen","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"found everywhere","is_correct":true},{"text":"recently invented","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$found everywhere$q$, $q$The examples, ocean trenches, mountain snow, and city air, show plastic turning up in every kind of place, so ubiquitous means found everywhere. "Found only in water" fits just one of the three examples.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('223251ea-7322-566a-be0b-cf2013bd03a9', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "To mitigate the damage from future storms, the town planted marsh grasses along the shore, which slow incoming waves before they reach the houses." As used in the sentence, "mitigate" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"predict","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"decorate","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"lessen","is_correct":true},{"text":"increase","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$lessen$q$, $q$The effect of the grasses, slowing waves before they reach the houses, shows the goal is to reduce harm, so mitigate means lessen. "Predict" sounds reasonable for storms, but planting grass does nothing to forecast them.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('f2ae99a4-d9b4-549f-907b-c193aca3697f', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The restorer was meticulous, spending an entire afternoon cleaning a single square inch of the painting with a cotton swab." As used in the sentence, "meticulous" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"hurried and careless with work","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"talented at painting portraits","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"extremely careful about details","is_correct":true},{"text":"nervous about making mistakes","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$extremely careful about details$q$, $q$The example of a whole afternoon spent on one square inch shows extreme care with detail. "Nervous about making mistakes" might seem likely, but the sentence describes her method, not her feelings.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('711bf9f8-0c65-5a03-a9ea-8b5f8910c97d', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Because the club's budget was tiny, the treasurer was frugal, reusing old poster board and buying supplies only when they were on sale." As used in the sentence, "frugal" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"skilled at making posters","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"eager to spend freely","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"unwilling to share ideas","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"careful not to waste money","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$careful not to waste money$q$, $q$The cause (a tiny budget) and the examples (reusing poster board, buying on sale) show careful spending, so frugal means careful not to waste money. "Skilled at making posters" picks up one detail but misses the point about money.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('5f232375-bb15-5409-9fe5-c730df158ee7', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "In a candid interview, the retired astronaut admitted that she had been terrified during her first launch and had nearly asked to stay home." As used in the sentence, "candid" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"recorded on video","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"honest and open","is_correct":true},{"text":"brief and guarded","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"funny and lighthearted","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$honest and open$q$, $q$The astronaut "admitted" a private fear, which shows she was speaking openly, so candid means honest and open. "Brief and guarded" is the opposite of someone sharing an embarrassing feeling.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('513e5d05-bcd4-5ec4-b52e-84261a987595', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Once digital cameras became cheap and common, the darkroom equipment in the school's basement was obsolete, and no one had touched it in years." As used in the sentence, "obsolete" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"stored in a basement or cellar","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"broken beyond any possible repair","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"brand new and in high demand","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"no longer used because something replaced it","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$no longer used because something replaced it$q$, $q$The cause clue, digital cameras becoming common, plus "no one had touched it in years" shows the equipment was replaced, so obsolete means no longer used. "Broken beyond any possible repair" is not supported, since nothing says the equipment stopped working.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('f51cf07b-b574-5ddf-9ea6-0654b20cccb1', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The biologist took a novel approach to counting bats: instead of using nets, she trained a computer to recognize each species by the sound of its calls." As used in the sentence, "novel" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"loud and disruptive","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"related to a long book","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"new and original","is_correct":true},{"text":"old and traditional","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$new and original$q$, $q$The contrast "instead of using nets" shows the biologist tried something new, so novel means new and original. A novel is also a long work of fiction, but that noun meaning does not fit a word describing an approach.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('381309fb-1a55-54ce-98a1-96098f223dda', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Before lending the company money, the bank wanted proof that its finances were sound, so it examined five years of records for any sign of trouble." As used in the sentence, "sound" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"stable and healthy","is_correct":true},{"text":"secret and private","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"failing and risky","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"loud and noticeable","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$stable and healthy$q$, $q$The bank checks the records "for any sign of trouble," so sound finances are stable and healthy. "Loud and noticeable" connects to sound as noise, a common meaning that has nothing to do with money.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('b089754e-829d-5c5a-af6f-ce68f74db399', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "With better irrigation, the same field that once produced a thin harvest now yields twice as much corn each summer." As used in the sentence, "yields" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"gives way to traffic","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"produces","is_correct":true},{"text":"waters","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"loses","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$produces$q$, $q$The restatement clue is "produced" earlier in the sentence: the field now yields more corn, so yields means produces. Drivers yield by giving way to traffic, but that meaning does not fit a field and its harvest.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('9ea39cb2-e48b-5a0c-a04a-445e07f86ad8', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Coral reefs can be surprisingly resilient; some that were badly bleached by warm water recovered their color within a few years once temperatures dropped." As used in the sentence, "resilient" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"able to recover from damage","is_correct":true},{"text":"easily destroyed for good","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"brightly and richly colored","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"found in warm, shallow water","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$able to recover from damage$q$, $q$The example after the semicolon shows bleached reefs that "recovered," so resilient means able to recover. "Brightly and richly colored" focuses on the color detail but misses the idea of bouncing back.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('b75316e4-79cd-56c7-ae84-365d3c876b9a', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Before signing the lease, Dev scrutinized every paragraph, reading the fine print twice and highlighting anything he did not understand." As used in the sentence, "scrutinized" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"rewrote completely","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"signed reluctantly","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"examined closely","is_correct":true},{"text":"glanced at quickly","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$examined closely$q$, $q$The examples of reading twice and highlighting show careful study, so scrutinized means examined closely. "Glanced at quickly" is the opposite of reading the fine print twice.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('874ea747-0bd3-561a-a5b0-90ab38c4f559', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'easy',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Unlike the textbook's confusing chapter, the teacher's explanation of photosynthesis was lucid, and every student could follow it on the first try." As used in the sentence, "lucid" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"confusing and hard to follow","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"related to plants and light","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"clear and easy to understand","is_correct":true},{"text":"long and full of detail","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$clear and easy to understand$q$, $q$The contrast with the "confusing chapter" and the result that "every student could follow it" show lucid means clear. "Long and full of detail" might describe a good lesson, but the sentence never mentions length.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('94282fae-8ec7-5670-a9e1-127da034a2f0', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The reviewer gave the new museum wing a qualified endorsement, praising its galleries while warning that the confusing floor plan left visitors lost." As used in the sentence, "qualified" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"having the proper training","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"completely enthusiastic","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"officially published","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"limited by reservations","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$limited by reservations$q$, $q$The praise comes with a warning about the floor plan, so the endorsement is held back by reservations. "Having the proper training" is the common meaning of qualified, as in a qualified nurse, but here the word describes an endorsement, not a person.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('b9b1e3b4-a479-50a4-886f-8a2bc1e0bb0d', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The theory that the bridge collapsed because of high winds does not hold, since weather records show the air was nearly still that night." As used in the sentence, "hold" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"grip firmly","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"remain valid","is_correct":true},{"text":"stay hidden","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"gain support","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$remain valid$q$, $q$The cause clue "since weather records show the air was nearly still" explains why the theory fails, so not hold means not remain valid. "Grip firmly" is the everyday physical meaning, which cannot apply to a theory.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('3f981db2-1537-56f4-9c65-121edd5070b3', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Early vaccination campaigns helped check the spread of the disease, and within a decade new cases had dropped to a handful each year." As used in the sentence, "check" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"record","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"inspect","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"restrain","is_correct":true},{"text":"speed up","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$restrain$q$, $q$The effect, cases dropping to a handful, shows the campaigns held the disease back, so check means restrain. "Inspect" is the most common meaning of check, but inspecting a spread would not make cases fall.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('d107e84b-052e-578e-91b1-2b971586a6e2', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Her command of Mandarin, Arabic, and Portuguese made her the obvious choice to guide the international delegation through the trade fair." As used in the sentence, "command" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"study","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"order","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"mastery","is_correct":true},{"text":"dislike","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$mastery$q$, $q$Her skill with three languages makes her "the obvious choice" to guide visitors, so command means mastery. "Order" is a common meaning of command, but no one gives an order to a language.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('f66228d0-c902-5d10-b2fe-d69a6d30dd3a', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "As houses that once belonged to the town's founders were restored, property values in the old district began to appreciate steadily." As used in the sentence, "appreciate" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"be inspected","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"fall in value","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"rise in value","is_correct":true},{"text":"feel grateful for","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$rise in value$q$, $q$The subject is property values, and restored houses make a neighborhood more desirable, so appreciate means rise in value. "Feel grateful for" is the familiar meaning, but values cannot feel anything.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('be21893c-8fb5-581b-8912-7cc2c140cb3f', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The judge ruled that the witness's comments about the defendant's clothing had no bearing on the case, which concerned only a missed contract payment." As used in the sentence, "bearing" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"weight","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"direction","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"appearance","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"relevance","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$relevance$q$, $q$The case "concerned only a missed contract payment," so comments about clothing have nothing to do with it, and bearing means relevance. A ship's bearing is its direction, a real meaning that does not fit a court ruling.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('8f72fa4e-76f4-56d2-a312-c3c5d8dfbd24', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Conservators sealed the ancient scrolls in an oxygen-free case, hoping to arrest the slow crumbling that had already claimed their edges." As used in the sentence, "arrest" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"halt","is_correct":true},{"text":"speed up","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"measure","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"take into custody","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$halt$q$, $q$Sealing the scrolls away from oxygen is meant to stop further damage, so arrest means halt. "Take into custody" is how police use the word, but crumbling is a process, not a person.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('eea00c4f-9eb2-528e-81ba-9533efe592a9', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The coach tempered her criticism with encouragement, pointing out the team's mistakes but also reminding them how much they had improved since September." As used in the sentence, "tempered" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"hardened by heating","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"intensified","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"softened","is_correct":true},{"text":"postponed","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$softened$q$, $q$Balancing criticism with reminders of improvement makes it gentler, so tempered means softened. Steel is tempered by heating to harden it, but that meaning does not apply to criticism and would suggest the opposite effect.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('474e1124-550a-5e2b-94f4-3f481ea30259', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "After hours of tense negotiation, the mayor's conciliatory tone, including an apology for the delay, finally persuaded the striking workers to return to the table." As used in the sentence, "conciliatory" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"intended to entertain a crowd","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"intended to calm and reconcile","is_correct":true},{"text":"intended to apologize only","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"intended to threaten and warn","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$intended to calm and reconcile$q$, $q$The apology and the result, workers returning to negotiate, show a tone meant to ease tension. "Intended to apologize only" is too narrow, since the apology is given as just one part of the tone.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('d8dedc53-0db9-5598-aebb-a5116e22b3da', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The fame of most viral videos is ephemeral; a clip watched by millions on Monday is often forgotten by the following weekend." As used in the sentence, "ephemeral" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"lasting for many generations","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"earning a great deal of money","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"lasting a very short time","is_correct":true},{"text":"spreading among young people","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$lasting a very short time$q$, $q$The explanation after the semicolon, popular on Monday and forgotten by the weekend, shows ephemeral means short-lived. "Earning a great deal of money" may seem connected to fame, but the sentence says nothing about money.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('bd5274b8-48cc-5dd9-abb9-e6ce76dd4193', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The anthology gathers disparate voices, from a medieval monk and a Victorian factory worker to a modern astronaut, who share almost nothing except a love of writing letters." As used in the sentence, "disparate" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"desperate for public attention","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"very different from one another","is_correct":true},{"text":"living in the same century","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"closely similar to one another","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$very different from one another$q$, $q$The examples span centuries and walks of life, and the writers "share almost nothing," so disparate means very different. "Desperate for public attention" plays on the similar spelling of desperate, and nothing in the sentence supports it.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('48e0fe46-59be-5eb0-94f6-97b673203576', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The scientist's careless errors in her earlier papers undermined the credibility of her new findings, even though the new study was carefully done." As used in the sentence, "undermined" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"published","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"dug beneath","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"strengthened","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"weakened","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$weakened$q$, $q$The contrast "even though the new study was carefully done" signals that the old errors hurt her reputation anyway, so undermined means weakened. "Dug beneath" is the literal meaning of undermine, but credibility is not something you can dig under.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('7cb5e467-7160-57ed-a544-ac1f8938734c', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The editor cut the third paragraph as superfluous, since it simply repeated points the essay had already made clearly." As used in the sentence, "superfluous" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"excellent","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"unnecessary","is_correct":true},{"text":"confusing","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"essential","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$unnecessary$q$, $q$The cause clue explains that the paragraph "simply repeated points" already made, so superfluous means unnecessary. "Confusing" is a reason editors cut text, but the sentence says the points were already made clearly.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('cd5fdeab-68b2-5c9e-9268-7426aaee46cf', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "News of the factory closing galvanized the quiet neighborhood; within a week, residents had formed a committee, held three meetings, and drafted a plan to attract new businesses." As used in the sentence, "galvanized" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"divided into groups","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"made discouraged","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"coated with zinc","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"spurred into action","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$spurred into action$q$, $q$The quick burst of activity after the semicolon shows the news stirred people to act, so galvanized means spurred into action. Steel is galvanized by coating it with zinc, a real meaning that does not fit a neighborhood.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('6248341e-c20f-5f69-bf7f-6c578757ee0b', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Perched on a narrow ledge with loose rocks below, the climbers' position was precarious, and a single slip could have sent them tumbling." As used in the sentence, "precarious" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"safe and secure","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"high above the ground","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"dangerously unstable","is_correct":true},{"text":"carefully planned","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$dangerously unstable$q$, $q$The narrow ledge, loose rocks, and the risk that "a single slip" could cause a fall show precarious means dangerously unstable. "High above the ground" captures only the height, not the danger the word describes.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('3560a8f7-c7d4-5819-901d-aa132e77aff9', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'medium',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Usually reticent in class, Theo surprised everyone by speaking for ten minutes about the history of the printing press." As used in the sentence, "reticent" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"inclined to dislike history","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"inclined to skip class","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"inclined to talk constantly","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"inclined to stay quiet","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$inclined to stay quiet$q$, $q$The word "surprised" sets up a contrast: speaking for ten minutes was unusual for Theo, so reticent means inclined to stay quiet. "Inclined to dislike history" is not supported; his quietness says nothing about how he feels about the subject.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('c7eea677-8599-5598-b558-bd1eaaa5500e', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'hard',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The pilot's face did not register the slightest alarm as the warning light flashed, and his calm voice kept the passengers from panicking." As used in the sentence, "register" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"show","is_correct":true},{"text":"hide","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"cause","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"enroll","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$show$q$, $q$A face that does "not register" alarm does not display it, and the calm voice confirms this, so register means show. "Enroll" is the common meaning, as in registering for a class, but a face cannot sign up for anything. "Hide" reverses the sentence, which says alarm was absent, not concealed.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('49ec14dd-b1b8-5da7-a072-0e9a8f5e6d50', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'hard',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Only the most discriminating tea buyers could tell the two harvests apart, noticing a faint smoky note that most drinkers missed entirely." As used in the sentence, "discriminating" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"unable to taste any difference","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"able to notice fine differences","is_correct":true},{"text":"treating people unfairly","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"willing to pay high prices","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$able to notice fine differences$q$, $q$These buyers catch "a faint smoky note that most drinkers missed," so discriminating means able to notice fine differences. "Treating people unfairly" is the most familiar use of discriminate, but the sentence is about tasting tea, not treating people. "Willing to pay high prices" is plausible for expert buyers, but the sentence never mentions cost.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('f64dec35-b068-5827-9ddf-6bfc83de7e05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'hard',
   $q$Read the sentence. "After a year of public hearings, the city council formally sanctioned the plan for a bike lane on Main Street, and construction crews arrived the following month." As used in the sentence, "sanctioned" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"penalized","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"approved","is_correct":true},{"text":"rejected","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"designed","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$approved$q$, $q$Construction began the next month, which only follows if the council gave permission, so sanctioned means approved. Sanction can also mean a penalty, as when nations impose sanctions, but a penalized plan would not lead to crews arriving. "Designed" fails because the council voted on the plan, not drew it up.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('37691c6f-1e49-5d77-b7a3-441c5f3d771b', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'hard',
   $q$Read the sentences. "When reporters asked whether the company would close the plant, the spokesperson gave an equivocal answer. 'Every option remains on the table,' she said, 'and we expect to make a decision eventually.'" As used in the sentence, "equivocal" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"clearly definite","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"completely false","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"openly hostile","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"deliberately vague","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$deliberately vague$q$, $q$The quoted reply commits to nothing, so equivocal means deliberately vague. "Completely false" is tempting because evasive answers can feel dishonest, but nothing shows her statement was untrue; it simply avoids a yes or no.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('7b91ed7a-2626-5118-9ca5-788ed12b762c', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'hard',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The explorer's journal was famously laconic; after crossing the frozen sea in forty days, his entire entry read, 'Arrived. Cold.'" As used in the sentence, "laconic" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"written in a foreign language","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"out-of-scope"},{"text":"using very few words","is_correct":true},{"text":"gloomy and complaining","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"full of vivid description","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$using very few words$q$, $q$The example after the semicolon, a two-word entry for a forty-day journey, shows laconic means using very few words. "Gloomy and complaining" might seem to fit the word "Cold," but one plain word is a report, not a complaint.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('d3fecf00-bb24-5858-8662-5285570b8f8f', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'hard',
   $q$Read the sentence. "Mountain weather is notoriously mercurial: a hiker can start out under a clear sky at dawn and be caught in hail before lunch." As used in the sentence, "mercurial" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"steady and easy to forecast","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"containing a silvery metal","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-word-meaning"},{"text":"changing suddenly and unpredictably","is_correct":true},{"text":"cold and often icy","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$changing suddenly and unpredictably$q$, $q$The colon introduces a quick shift from clear sky to hail, so mercurial means changing suddenly. "Containing a silvery metal" connects the word to mercury, its root, but weather contains no metal. "Cold and often icy" fits only the hail detail and misses the sudden change.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('7efc88ca-b2bc-5faf-a19b-bc61307accc7', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'hard',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The inspector's review of the elevator was perfunctory; he glanced at the cables for a few seconds, signed the form, and left without testing the brakes." As used in the sentence, "perfunctory" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"done illegally, for a secret payment","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"done slowly and with great attention","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"done by checking only the cables","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"done quickly as a routine, without care","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$done quickly as a routine, without care$q$, $q$The examples, a glance of a few seconds and skipping the brakes, show a careless, routine job, so perfunctory means done quickly without care. "Done illegally, for a secret payment" is a tempting story to imagine, but the sentence gives no evidence of a bribe.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb),
  ('12f0de3b-4559-522d-b712-3a4dc0dc4c09', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-vocabulary-in-context'), 'hard',
   $q$Read the sentence. "The composer's idiosyncratic habits, such as writing only on green paper and refusing to work before midnight, puzzled his assistants but never slowed his output." As used in the sentence, "idiosyncratic" most nearly means:$q$, $q$[{"text":"harmful to productivity","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"shared by most musicians","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"peculiar to one individual","is_correct":true},{"text":"related to color choices","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$peculiar to one individual$q$, $q$The odd examples, green paper and working only after midnight, show habits unique to this one composer. "Harmful to productivity" might seem to follow from strange habits, but the sentence says they "never slowed his output." "Related to color choices" covers only the green paper.$q$,
   'text', null::jsonb)
  ) as v (id, skill_id, difficulty, stem, choices, correct_answer, solution, render_mode, prompt)
